+### 0052 — BUG-260823-1vx45a: managerlock subprocess results preceded lock release
+
+- ROOT CAUSE: `TestManagerLockHelper` registered acquired `Operation` and `HomeLock` handles with `testing.T.Cleanup`, then printed `acquired` or `blocked` before those callbacks ran. `TestSubprocessBuildKeyDeduplicationAcrossProjects` likewise left its parent operation to deferred test cleanup. Unix permits unlinking an open file and hid the lifecycle gap; Windows keeps `.lock` files undeletable while either process still owns a handle, so `t.TempDir` cleanup could fail after the assertions passed.
+- FIX AND REGRESSION SHAPE: helper branches now close every lock explicitly before publishing their result, including the normal `hold-project` EOF path, and the build-key test closes its parent operation in the test body. The final helper invocation in both affected tests uses an opt-in handshake: the child releases its lock, prints the result, and remains alive while the parent removes the manager home. On Windows the old cleanup-deferred lifecycle makes that removal fail deterministically; the corrected lifecycle permits it without moving state out of `t.TempDir` or suppressing cleanup errors.
+- CURRENT-BYTE EVIDENCE: macOS named tests 10x, package 5x, and named race tests 5x exited 0; Linux/arm64 Go 1.25.5 named tests 10x and package 5x exited 0; Windows/amd64 test-binary compilation exited 0; `go vet ./...`, `golangci-lint run`, build, and diff hygiene exited 0. Native Windows execution was not available because the online Tailscale Windows host rejected every loaded public key for `administrator`; five consecutive Windows CI runs therefore remain a post-publication gate for the commit/PR-owning orchestrator.
